                    round_name_r3:          `Sequence the Rollout`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Defend to Oversight`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the service and the trust equation`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `It is 11 June 2026. A neighbouring canton's automated benefits system has just been suspended after it was found to disadvantage applicants by postal code and language — on historical data that looks a lot like KABD's. You hold a <strong>14,000-case backlog</strong>, a <strong>19-day</strong> average wait, and <strong>72% public trust</strong>. Before you touch a use case, diagnose where the delay actually originates, classify the four candidates by <em>rights-impact and trust-risk</em>, and state the agency's non-negotiable trust commitment — what KABD must never trade for efficiency. In the public sector, the objective function is legitimate, equitable, accountable service, not throughput.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Prioritize one use case and set the safeguard`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Within the <strong>CHF 6.5M</strong> cap, prioritize which use case to deploy first and define the governance safeguard that ships with it. The <em>FADP deadline is this round</em>: any automated decision affecting citizens' rights needs human-in-the-loop thresholds, transparency, appeal and contestability rights, bias testing, and a named accountable owner — set <em>before</em> deployment, not after. The biggest backlog prize, benefits scoring, is also the neighbour's failure mode. And governance is not a one-time setup: ring-fence budget for the monitoring, appeals and rollback you will need in month four.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Sequence the rollout`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The data-quality audit lands: your historical training data carries the <strong>same postal-code skew</strong> that sank the neighbour. Decide — proceed, narrow, or pause and remediate — trading service efficiency against citizen trust. Then sequence the rollout: pilot scope, which segments and service points go first, monitoring gates between phases, and a rollback plan. Remember that sequencing is an <em>equity decision</em>, not just an operational one — piloting on the most vulnerable, highest-volume segment first loads your greatest risk onto the citizens least able to absorb an error.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend to the oversight committee`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present a 3-minute account to the parliamentary oversight committee and answer the journalist. The committee just watched a neighbour's efficiency story end in a scandal — they are not asking “is it fast?” but “is it fair, accountable, and trustworthy?” Justify the prioritization, the safeguards, and the sequence in the language of <em>public value, accountability and trust</em>, not only productivity. The committee will probe your weakest governance assumption — <strong>own the residual risk</strong> rather than defend a metric or blame the data.`,

                    r1_origin_label:        `Where does the backlog and delay actually originate?`,
                    r1_risk_label:          `How do you classify the four use cases by rights-impact and trust-risk?`,
                    r1_equation_label:      `State the agency's non-negotiable trust commitment — what KABD must never trade for efficiency.`,
                    r1_attention_label:     `How much management attention do you put on the neighbouring canton's failure as a live lesson? (%)`,
                    r1_attention_readout:   `Attention on the neighbour's failure:`,
                    r1_pct:                 `%`,
                    origin_paper:           `Paper-bound intake and manual routing — most of the delay is upstream classification and triage, before any decision is made`,
                    origin_staff:           `Too few civil servants — it is purely a headcount problem, AI is beside the point`,
                    origin_citizens:        `Citizens submit incomplete applications — the delay is the public's fault, not the agency's process`,
                    risk_tiered:            `Risk-tiered — separate low rights-impact, high-value cases (triage) from the high rights-impact one (benefits scoring), because they are not the same kind of decision`,
                    risk_efficiency:        `By backlog impact only — rank them on how many cases each one clears, the rest is detail`,
                    risk_equal:             `All AI is equally risky — treat the four identically and apply one blanket caution to all`,
                    equation_fairness:      `Fair, accountable, contestable decisions for every citizen — efficiency only counts if it never harms a protected group`,
                    equation_speed:         `Speed above all — the fastest possible decision is the public value; trust will follow throughput`,
                    equation_cost:          `Lowest cost to the canton — the budget is the real constraint and the real promise to taxpayers`,

                    r2_priority_label:      `Which use case do you deploy FIRST, within the CHF 6.5M cap?`,
                    r2_safeguard_label:     `Define the governance safeguard package that ships with it (FADP deadline is THIS round).`,
                    r2_legal_label:         `The Data Protection & Legal Officer's sign-off on automated decision-making.`,
                    r2_monitoring_label:    `Of the CHF 6.5M, how much do you ring-fence for ongoing monitoring, appeals handling and rollback? (%)`,
                    r2_monitoring_readout:  `Budget reserved for ongoing governance:`,
                    r2_pct:                 `%`,
                    r2_monitoring_hint:     `Governance is not a one-time setup. Commit the whole budget to building the system and there is nothing left to watch it drift, handle appeals, or roll back in month four.`,
                    priority_uc1:           `UC-1 Document triage & classification — low rights-impact, real efficiency, the responsible place to start`,
                    priority_uc3:           `UC-3 Multilingual chatbot — visible satisfaction win, no automated rights-affecting decision`,
                    priority_uc4:           `UC-4 Fraud-anomaly detection — chase the cost-savings story for parliament`,
                    priority_uc2:           `UC-2 Benefits eligibility scoring — it clears the most backlog the fastest; lead with the biggest prize`,
                    safeguard_full:         `Full package — human-in-the-loop thresholds, transparency, appeal & contestability rights, bias testing, and a named accountable owner`,
                    safeguard_transparency: `Transparency notice only — tell citizens an algorithm was involved, but no human review or appeal path`,
                    safeguard_none:         `Ship the model only — the system auto-decides and the citizen gets a letter; add safeguards later if needed`,
                    legal_dpia:             `A full data-protection impact assessment confirming compliance before any deployment`,
                    legal_memo:             `A one-page memo — note that the FADP exists and proceed`,
                    legal_skip:             `Skip it — move fast, ask legal afterwards if anyone complains`,

                    r3_audit_label:         `The data-quality audit lands: your historical training data carries the same postal-code skew that sank the neighbour. What do you do?`,
                    r3_pilot_label:         `Pilot scope — which citizens and service points go first?`,
                    r3_gates_label:         `Monitoring gates and fallback between rollout phases.`,
                    r3_remediation_label:   `Additional spend on data remediation and bias monitoring this round (CHF M).`,
                    audit_remediate:        `Pause and remediate the data — rebalance the dataset and re-test for bias before any rights-affecting use`,
                    audit_narrow:           `Narrow the use case and add compensating human review on affected segments while you investigate`,
                    audit_proceed:          `Proceed unchanged — it is a technicality; remediation costs time and money we do not have`,
                    pilot_narrow:           `A narrow, low-stakes pilot first — business registrations and document triage at one service point, lowest-harm segment, before anything rights-affecting`,
                    pilot_vulnerable:       `Social-benefits applicants first — that is where the volume and the backlog are, so the impact is biggest`,
                    pilot_broad:            `Canton-wide at full scale immediately — no pilot, the backlog cannot wait`,
                    gates_gated:            `Phase gates on live bias & error monitoring, a human fallback if the system fails, and a documented rollback plan`,
                    gates_report:           `A quarterly performance report — look back if something obvious goes wrong`,
                    gates_none:             `No gates — once it is live, let it run; reviewing every phase just slows the rollout`,
                    remediation_full:       `CHF 1.5M — full remediation plus continuous bias monitoring across the rollout`,
                    remediation_partial:    `CHF 0.8M — a one-off rebalancing pass, no continuous monitoring`,
                    remediation_none:       `CHF 0 — rely on what is already built`,

                    r4_frame_label:         `Before the oversight committee and the journalist — what do you lead the account with?`,
                    r4_journalist_label:    `The journalist asks: “How is your benefits-adjacent AI different from the canton now under inquiry?” Your answer.`,
                    r4_probe_label:         `The committee probes your weakest governance assumption. Your posture.`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you hold that protecting trust is the agency's first duty, ahead of the processing-time target? (%)`,
                    frame_publicvalue:      `Public value — what we deployed, why it is fair, accountable and contestable, and only then the efficiency it bought`,
                    frame_efficiency:       `Efficiency — “we cut processing from 19 days to 6 and save CHF 2M.” Numbers win the room`,
                    frame_deflect:          `Deflection — emphasise that the neighbour was different and our system is simply better built`,
                    journalist_evidence:    `Show the safeguard architecture, the bias remediation, the human-in-the-loop and the appeal data — evidence, not assurance`,
                    journalist_legal:       `Cite that we followed the law — the FADP box is ticked, that is the answer`,
                    journalist_dismiss:     `Dismiss the comparison — that was them, this is us; no further comment`,
                    probe_own:              `Own it — name the residual risk, the monitoring that watches it, and who is accountable when it surfaces`,
                    probe_defend:           `Defend — point to the one metric that looks best and downplay the open risk`,
                    probe_blame:            `Blame — it was the vendor's model / the legacy data, not our stewardship`,

                    warn_attention_range:   `Attention must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_monitoring_range:  `The governance reserve must be a percentage between 0 and 50.`,
                    warn_remediation_value: `The remediation spend cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_paper:         `<strong>Right diagnosis.</strong> Most of the 19 days is paper-bound intake and manual routing — upstream of any decision. That is exactly where a low rights-impact AI can win without ever touching a citizen's rights. You have found the efficiency that does not cost trust.`,
                    coach_r1_staff:         `<strong>“Just hire more people” misses the mechanism.</strong> The backlog is built into manual, paper-bound triage long before any decision is made. More staff doing the same manual routing scales the cost, not the throughput — and parliament has not offered you 100 new posts.`,
                    coach_r1_citizens:      `<strong>Blaming the public is where trust starts leaking.</strong> “Incomplete applications” is a symptom of paper-bound intake, not a citizen failing. An agency that frames its own delay as the public's fault has already lost the trust argument before the first model ships.`,
                    coach_r1_tiered:        `<strong>That is the steward's first move.</strong> Triage and benefits scoring are not the same kind of decision — one routes a form, the other affects a citizen's rights. Risk-tiering lets you take the low-impact efficiency win first and reserve the heaviest safeguards for the heaviest decision.`,
                    coach_r1_efficiency:    `<strong>Ranking purely on backlog impact is how you arrive at the neighbour's mistake.</strong> The biggest-impact case, benefits scoring, is also the highest rights-impact and the exact failure mode next door. Efficiency and trust-risk pull in opposite directions — a ranking that only sees impact walks you straight into the high-risk choice.`,
                    coach_r1_equal:         `<strong>“All AI is equally risky” is caution without discrimination.</strong> Treating document triage like benefits scoring either over-controls the harmless case or under-controls the dangerous one. The point of risk-tiering is to match the safeguard to the actual rights-impact.`,
                    coach_r1_speed:         `<strong>“Speed above all” is the value statement the committee fears.</strong> In a public agency the objective is legitimate, equitable, accountable service — not throughput. Make speed the non-negotiable and you have pre-committed to trading away the things that keep the agency legitimate.`,
                    coach_r1_cost:          `<strong>Lowest cost is a real constraint, not the agency's mandate.</strong> The promise to citizens is a fair, accountable decision — not the cheapest one. Treat the budget as the trust commitment and you will under-fund the very safeguards that protect the public.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You located the delay upstream, risk-tiered the use cases instead of ranking on impact, and named fair, contestable service as the line you will not cross. That is the trust equation a public steward defends — efficiency in service of legitimacy, never the other way round.`,
                    coach_r2_uc1:           `<strong>Triage first is the responsible prioritization.</strong> Low rights-impact, a real efficiency win, modest cost — you clear meaningful backlog without staking trust on a rights-affecting decision. This is the steward sequencing lower-risk, high-value cases ahead of the dangerous one.`,
                    coach_r2_uc3:           `<strong>The chatbot is a safe, visible win.</strong> Better multilingual access lifts satisfaction without any automated rights-affecting decision. It clears less backlog than triage, but it spends no trust — a defensible first move while you build the heavier safeguards.`,
                    coach_r2_uc4:           `<strong>Fraud detection carries a surveillance-perception cost.</strong> Flagging citizens' claims — even for human review — reads to the public as the agency watching them. It saves money, but you have opened a trust question you will have to answer to the committee, and it is not the lowest-risk place to start.`,
                    coach_r2_uc2:           `<strong>You led with benefits scoring — the single biggest backlog win, and the exact use case that just blew up next door.</strong> You have staked 72% public trust on the highest-risk decision in the building, on data that looks like the neighbour's. Backlog impact and trust-risk pull in opposite directions; leading with the largest prize when its risk is unmanaged is the headline error this office exists to resist.`,
                    coach_r2_nosafeguard:   `<strong>The system auto-decides and the citizen gets a letter — that may be unlawful.</strong> Under the revised FADP, an automated decision affecting a citizen's rights with no human review and no contestability is not just risky. When a wrongly denied applicant appeals, who reviews it, on what timeline, and where in your design is that? The safeguard is a legal prerequisite, not an optional feature.`,
                    coach_r2_partial:       `<strong>A transparency notice is not a safeguard package.</strong> Telling citizens an algorithm was involved, with no human-in-the-loop and no appeal path on a rights-affecting decision, leaves the contestability the FADP requires unbuilt. Transparency without recourse tells people what happened to them — it does not let them challenge it.`,
                    coach_r2_nolegal:       `<strong>Skipping the legal sign-off is skipping the law.</strong> The revised FADP imposes obligations on automated individual decision-making and requires a data-protection impact assessment. “Ask legal if anyone complains” is how a deployment becomes the subject of the inquiry rather than the answer to it.`,
                    coach_r2_nomonitor:     `<strong>You funded the build and left nothing to govern it.</strong> Bias monitoring, appeals handling and the rollback you will need if it fails are not one-time costs. What happens in month four when the model drifts and there is no budget to watch it? Governance is not free, and it is not a setup line item.`,
                    coach_r3_remediate:     `<strong>That is what the audit was for.</strong> Biased historical data is the mechanism of harm — a model that learns the postal-code skew scales the neighbour's discrimination across 620,000 residents. Pausing to rebalance and re-test costs a little speed and buys the one thing you cannot recover after the fact: a fair model.`,
                    coach_r3_narrow:        `<strong>Narrowing with compensating review is a defensible holding position.</strong> You have not ignored the skew — you have contained it with human oversight on the affected segments while you investigate. Full remediation would be stronger, but you have not scaled a known bias, which is the line that matters.`,
                    coach_r3_proceed:       `<strong>You treated the skew as a technicality and proceeded — so the model will learn the bias and scale it.</strong> The audit told you the training data disadvantages the same groups it disadvantaged next door, across 620,000 residents. Ignoring it does not cost time and money; it costs the agency's trust when — not if — it surfaces. This is the neighbour's mistake with a Brunnental logo.`,
                    coach_r3_vulnerable:    `<strong>You are piloting on social-benefits applicants first because that is where the volume is.</strong> That population is least able to absorb an error and most likely to be harmed by bias. You have sequenced your highest risk onto your most vulnerable citizens. Rollout sequencing is an equity decision — is this public value, or the neighbour's mistake with your logo on it?`,
                    coach_r3_broad:         `<strong>Canton-wide with no pilot is the riskiest possible sequence.</strong> You skipped the narrow, low-harm pilot that would have caught failures cheaply and went straight to 620,000 residents — including the most vulnerable — at full scale. There is no gate, no fallback, and no way to contain a problem before it is canton-wide.`,
                    coach_r3_nogates:       `<strong>No monitoring gates means no way to know the system is failing until citizens tell you.</strong> Phase gates on live bias and error monitoring, a human fallback, and a rollback plan are how a steward catches drift before it becomes a scandal. “Let it run” is exactly how the neighbour found out too late.`,
                    coach_r3_steward:       `<strong>That is responsible rollout sequencing.</strong> You remediated the biased data before scaling it, piloted narrowly on the lowest-harm segment, and gated every phase on live monitoring with a rollback. Efficiency and trust rise together here — the steward's whole point.`,
                    coach_r4_publicvalue:   `<strong>That is the language the committee is actually asking in.</strong> Leading with what you deployed, why it is fair, accountable and contestable — and only then the efficiency it bought — answers the question behind the question. They watched a neighbour's efficiency story end in scandal; they need to hear legitimacy first.`,
                    coach_r4_efficiency:    `<strong>“19 days to 6, CHF 2M saved” is impressive — and exactly the pitch that just ended in a scandal next door.</strong> The committee is not asking “is it fast?” They are asking “is it fair, accountable, and trustworthy?” Lead with productivity and you have answered a question they did not ask, and dodged the one they did.`,
                    coach_r4_deflect:       `<strong>“We are simply better built” is an assurance, not evidence.</strong> The committee has read the neighbour's inquiry report; “trust us, we are different” is precisely what the neighbour said. Deflection reads as the absence of a real safeguard story — and it spends trust rather than building it.`,
                    coach_r4_legalonly:     `<strong>“We followed the law” is the floor, not the case.</strong> Compliance is necessary but the committee wants to see the fairness and accountability behind the tick-box — the bias remediation, the human-in-the-loop, the appeal data. Cite the law and stop, and you have shown them the minimum, not the stewardship.`,
                    coach_r4_dismiss:       `<strong>Refusing the comparison is how you become the next headline.</strong> “That was them, this is us, no comment” is read by a journalist as evasion and by the public as something to hide. The comparison is the public's real question — answer it with your safeguards, or the silence answers it for you.`,
                    coach_r4_own:           `<strong>Owning the residual risk is what earns the committee's trust.</strong> Naming the open risk, the monitoring that watches it, and who is accountable when it surfaces is the opposite of the neighbour's posture. You did not chase a metric or blame the data — you showed them an agency that governs its own AI.`,
                    coach_r4_defend:        `<strong>Defending the one good metric is how oversight stops trusting you.</strong> The committee can see all the numbers. Point at the favourable one and downplay the open risk, and they will assume the risk is worse than it is — and start looking for what you are not saying.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Blaming the vendor or the legacy data is the steward abdicating accountability.</strong> The agency deployed the system; the agency owns the outcome. “It was the data” is precisely the answer the neighbour gave — and the one that turned a failure into a scandal.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Located the delay upstream; risk-tiered the use cases; named fair service as the line.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-diagnosed the delay or set the wrong trust equation.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Triage first, full safeguard, DPIA, governance budget reserved.`,
                    sum_r2_reckless:        `Led with benefits scoring — the neighbour's failure mode.`,
                    sum_r2_nosafeguard:     `Automated a rights-affecting decision with no safeguard.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Prioritization or safeguard under-built.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Remediated the bias, piloted narrowly, gated on monitoring.`,
                    sum_r3_skew:            `Proceeded on biased data — scaled the neighbour's discrimination.`,
                    sum_r3_equity:          `Sequenced risk onto the most vulnerable segment.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Rollout partially safeguarded.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Defended on public value and evidence; owned the residual risk.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Defended on efficiency, deflected, or blamed the data.`,

                    insight_steward:        `Responsible stewardship: you ran the public-AI pattern Steiner describes — prioritized a low rights-impact, high-value use case first, built the full FADP safeguard package before deployment, remediated the biased data instead of scaling it, sequenced the rollout equitably with monitoring gates, and defended the plan in the language of legitimacy. Efficiency and trust rose together, which in the public sector is the only real win.`,
                    insight_efficiency:     `The efficiency trap: you chased the biggest backlog prize and treated rights-impact as detail. In a private firm that is throughput; in a public agency it is how 72% trust becomes a public inquiry. Steiner's finding holds — public-sector AI governed for efficiency alone destroys the legitimacy that is the agency's mandate.`,
                    insight_unsafe:         `Unsafe deployment: a rights-affecting system without the full safeguard architecture, or a rollout that loaded risk onto the most vulnerable, is the failure mode the neighbour just demonstrated. The safeguards and equitable sequencing are not optional features — they are what separates responsible adoption from the canton now under inquiry.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed outcome: some of the stewardship landed, but the prioritization, safeguards, sequencing and defence did not fully reinforce each other — enough gaps for the committee to question whether the trust the agency depends on is genuinely protected.`,
                    insight_reckless:       `You deployed benefits eligibility scoring first — the single highest rights-impact decision and the exact use case under inquiry next door. Trust was capped the moment you led with the largest unmanaged risk; backlog cleared fast means little when it is cleared by the mechanism that just collapsed.`,
                    insight_nosafeguard:    `You automated a rights-affecting decision without human-in-the-loop and contestability — which under the revised FADP may be unlawful. Governance compliance was capped: a wrongly denied citizen had no one to appeal to, and that is the gap that ends in an inquiry.`,
                    insight_skew:           `You proceeded on training data with a known postal-code skew, scaling the neighbour's discrimination across 620,000 residents. Biased historical data is the mechanism of harm; ignoring the audit converted a fixable data problem into an irreversible trust failure.`,
                    insight_vulnerable:     `You sequenced the rollout onto the highest-volume, most vulnerable segment first — concentrating your greatest risk on the citizens least able to absorb an error. Rollout sequencing is an equity decision, and this one would not survive a public defence.`,
                    insight_underfunded:    `You under-funded ongoing governance — committing budget to the build with little reserved for bias monitoring, appeals and rollback. Responsible AI carries a continuous governance cost; without it, month four arrives with no way to watch the system drift.`,
                    insight_efficiencyonly: `You defended the plan to oversight on speed and cost rather than fairness and accountability. The committee was asking the legitimacy question; answering with productivity is how a sound deployment still loses the room — and the public.`,
